This was actually my second ascension, although the first one was in
1994, a Samurai, but murky enough and so long ago that this feels like
my first "real" ascension, and is anyway my first AP. Apologies if this
post is long and scattered for being a very straightforward valk
ascension.

We begin our story with our heroine already in the Dungeons of
Doom.

The valkyrie feels very pleased with herself for finding crystal
platemail on the floor of a second level room. A scroll from a nearby
bookstore lets her know she already has picked up a ring of
slow digestion and three nice scrolls of charging (pre-blessed!),
enchant weapon, and genocide.  "Wow," she thinks, "It must suck
to be someone who finds just polearms."

She continues to find quite a few rings, marvelling at her luck. In
the middle of trying out one of these rings -- phomph! she changes
into a baby black dragon. Armor breaks away in shards, and her
equipment clatters to the floor, to tumble down the stairs that she
happens to be standing at the head of. The RNG snickers. She
recovers however and knocks off Sokoban where she gets this
magic bag that lets her carry a lot but not quite as much as she
wants, like it knows.

The Dungeons go down and down but she knows she must head back
to the Gnomish Mines. Minetown is a dark grotto, with bones from
sauvaigne and nailbunny there. Nothing too useful in the bones; the
altar is coaligned and she gets some holy water before going
down to get the luckstone, then back up, finding a magic marker
on the way. She soon tires of the town and heads back up to
the main dungeon. "So long, Dull-halla" she sneers.

On level 9: You read "Vlad was here" --more--
You see here 2 cream pies.
("Well that was thoughtful," she thinks.)

She gets a little scared of a chameleon threatening her until
it turns into pony and she tames it with a thrown fruit. Names
it Shifty. At level 12 she tromps down the stairs and finds herself
standing in a huge room with weird lighting. Right at the foot of
the stairs she's surrounded by monsters and the lower steps
are covered with a blood smear and a lot of random equipment.
There's a ghost of some tapin fellow among the monsters, looking
bitter.

Someone screams "Get her!" and she grabs the bag of holding
on the ground, splashes it with eau d'Odin, and runs upstairs.

The bag has the usual odds and ends, but also Excalibur,
Mojo, and Frostbrand. "Thank you Odin, and tapin" she mutters,
rolling a rock onto the lesser weapons as she takes practice
swings with the grim freezing blade, and then spends awhile
blessing it, running back to minetown, getting more water,
using a magic marker she picked up in the mines, etc. Finally
she's ready to go back into the battle, and descends back
into the bigroom.

The monsters stop chatting with one another and look up.
"There she is again! Get her!"  Swinging the two longswords
she makes rather medium-length work of them. A lich drops
in. An angry xorn looms behind her! But Shifty, who up until
now has been a major pain in the ass, turns into a sphere
of some kind and goes *pop* at the xorn, not really saving
the valkyrie's life, but it's something useful at least,
so she likes to remember it respectfully.

In the immense chamber she finds a strange polymorphing
field. A python she releases from a figurine jumps in first,
transforming into a minotaur named Hugo. The valkyrie's pet
kitten turns tail and runs, mewling, "stop... whistling..
why.. do.. you.. have." --pzott--, out of the field steps a
well-dressed master lich. "Well, this is unusual. Call
me Boudewijn."

Hugo and Boudewijn agree to accompany the valkyrie on some
errands. Hugo seems to have made a deal where he gets to
eat all the shopkeepers he wants (-1), but Boudwijn is above that.
"Lo," he tells the valkyrie, pausing for a moment to
cover some random passerby in frost, "I know what you seek,
but you lack a way across the waters, and further lack the
power to reflect the deadly harm that awaits you there."

The valkyrie frowns. "Remember Lord Surtur?" she asks.
"Not really," replies Boudewijn. "Oh, that big one who
gave you that fascinating Orb." "Well, I think since we
did that so easily, we can do the castle."

Boudewijn seems unsure but Hugo is game. Down to medusa, then
at the edge of the waters, the valkyrie waves a wand, falling
down the hole that appears beneath her feet. Boudewijn
and Hugo shrug and jump down too since they're so close.

Hugo grunts excitedly. "A labyrinth!" he bellows. Right after
he scampers off another well dressed master lich appears,
nods cordially to Boudewijn, then sees the valkyrie. "This one's
mine," he says, but soon is sent fleeing up the stairs. The
valkyrie is about to chase him up but Boudewijn clears his throat.
"Not a good idea."

She admits this, and they run off to find Hugo, who has found
a moat filled with sea life with whom to play his game of
"do I kill you before you get to counter-attack?"  The valkyrie
joins him in the courtyard before the strange subterranean
castle, and cracks her knuckles.

The valkyrie reads a scroll and boulders drop from the ceiling
to fill the moat, then she waves a wand to splinter the drawbridge.
"Amateur," snorts Boudewijn. As the human and minotaur jump into the
fortress gleefully, Boudewijn teleports around, yawning, covering
things
with frost trying to look useful, although the screaming and roaring
and death-wails are amusing enough. Soon enough the castle is well
sacked and the valkyrie is twirling a wand looking smug. Boudewijn
blinks at the gleaming form of the valkyrie, whose silvery mail causes
the air about her to shimmer as she floats above the ground.

Hugo lies on his back on the floor, breathing hard and rubbing
his stomach.

The valkyrie pushes past Boudewijn to the stairs up. Hugo hears a
whistle and walks up the stairs after the valkyrie. He barely has time
to rub his eyes before the ugliest food-creature he's ever seen
turns him into a statue with a glance.

Boudewijn chuckles as the valkyrie comes back downstairs. "What,
no Hugo, victor?"  "Shut up!" the valkyrie answers. Boudewijn
shakes his head. "I suppose you dealt with the mistress of the
house, and left that other master lich up there."  The valkyrie
doesn't answer, and instead leads him down another hole, despite
his warnings of precipitate behavior. An unknown wand with no
obvious effect is very quickly found, and behold, it does turn
out to be just the thing to return Hugo to his fleshy form.

"You are the luckiest fool alive," comments Boudewijn, to no
one particular companion of his. The three heroes take a last
look around the shattered castle and head back down. Hugo stops
for a snack of a priest before they plunge into the endless
cruel mazes of Gehennom, where unspeakable cruelty awaits.
"Wake me up when we hit the sanctum" mutters Boudewijn.

.. Boudewijn awakes with a start. "Careful, I nearly covered
you in deadly, deadly frost." Boudewijn looks around at the
clearing formed -- by the Ritual, he must suppose.
Labyrinth walls have buckled into fire and water and rubble.
Hugo bellows happily as small green creatures jump from the
water in countless numbers. Some Hugo smashes. Some make
it past him, running for the valkyrie only to perish in gouts
of flame around her, where the ground is piled high with
their corpses.  She drops the heavy ritual artifacts
with relief and heads down the stairs. Hugo follows. Boudewijn
stops on his way, picking up an ornate candelabrum. "This
will look good in my sepulchre."

Soon after they emerge from the sanctum with the amulet.
The valkyrie looks a little embarrassed. Hugo points out that
even if Moloch hadn't kept hitting his own high priest with
lightning bolts, it was obvious the valkyrie would have won
anyway. Boudewijn tries to keep the candelabrum under his
robe but it keeps falling to the floor.

Up they go, bothered only a little by a mysterious force.
Boudewijn tries to get more sleep but some type in purple
keeps showing up with the mistaken impression that the amulet
belongs to him, how he traded it for some book but it was
just temporary, on loan really, etc.  They escape Gehennom,
going up and up through the many rooms and twisty hallways,
until the magic of the amulet transports them into the
dimensions beyond and above the real world.

In a realm of caverns Hugo is left behind, although he
seems pleased enough there. In a bizarre watery realm Boudewijn
parts company, still carrying his candelabrum, looking
unsure how he's going to get home. Finally the valkyrie
arrives at the astral plane, alone and battered, with
maybe a little debris still stuck in her hair. On the astral
she is as close to the gods as one may be while yet mortal,
and after some confusion over which hallway to go down (twice)
she reaches the holiest altar of Odin and offers the amulet,
and is taken into the heavens to serve by Odin's side, probably
in some sort of auxiliary smiting capacity.



